  <section id="warning-signs" class="section-compact section-rule" aria-labelledby="animals-signs-title" data-pagefind-body>
    <div class="shell animals-facts-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">What to watch for</p>
        <h2 id="animals-signs-title">Possible warning signs.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="article-body">
        <p>Poisoning can look different depending on the substance. Weakness, poor coordination, unusual breathing, loss of appetite or bleeding in a pet warrant prompt veterinary advice. Symptoms may not appear immediately.</p>
        <p>UnPoison’s original guide asks people to look for wildlife sitting out in broad daylight or in the same place for hours, birds of prey on the ground rather than in trees, unusually tame behaviour, lethargy, disorientation, weak flight or an inability to fly. It also notes that very weak animals may sit near bird baths or pools.</p>
        <p>These signs are not a diagnosis. Contact a wildlife rehabilitator or vet before approaching an animal; only attempt to contain a bird if a professional advises it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="secondary-poisoning" class="section-compact section-rule animals-pathway-band" aria-labelledby="animals-pathway-title">
    <div class="shell animals-facts-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The wider impact</p>
        <h2 id="animals-pathway-title">Poison can move through the food chain.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="article-body">
        <p>Animals may consume poison bait directly. Predators and scavengers can also be harmed after eating a rodent that has consumed bait—known as secondary poisoning.</p>
        <ol class="animals-pathway-list">
          <li>A rodent eats poisoned bait.</li>
          <li>An owl, raptor or another animal eats the rodent.</li>
          <li>The predator may be exposed to the poison as well.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Dogs, cats and other wildlife can be exposed too. The original page names owls, eagles, falcons, hawks, mongooses, genets, meerkats, jackals, foxes, herons, gulls, crows, Cape clawless otters and wildcats among animals of concern. Effects can be delayed and may affect an animal’s ability to hunt or survive. Different products have different effects, so identifying a suspected product and speaking to a veterinarian matters.</p>
        <a class="line-link" href="https://youtube.com/shorts/p5dA7RdPmxo?feature=share" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Watch the owl-poisoning video <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span><span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

