  <section id="prevention" class="section-compact section-rule" aria-labelledby="animals-prevention-title" data-pagefind-body>
    <div class="shell animals-facts-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Preventing harm</p>
        <h2 id="animals-prevention-title">Start by removing the attraction.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="article-body">
        <p>The original Animals page starts with sanitation: remove accessible food, keep edible business waste in sealed containers, clear food from around bins, and use enclosed compost systems for kitchen scraps. It also advises removing bird feeders where spilled seed attracts rats.</p>
        <p>It recommends enclosed T-REX snap traps as a non-poison option, distinguishing larger American and smaller Chinese versions. Its central safety point is that a trap must sit inside a suitable bait station so cats, ground birds and children cannot reach it. Ask a qualified pest-control professional about an appropriate, humane setup for your situation.</p>
        <p class="fine-print">These trapping options appeared on UnPoison’s previous website; ask a qualified pest-control professional what is appropriate and safe locally. This guide is not a substitute for veterinary care. For further background, see the <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/special-pet-topics/poisoning/rodenticide-poisoning">Merck Veterinary Manual’s overview of rodenticide poisoning</a> and the <a href="https://nspca.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2021/11/5.3-Animal-poisons.pdf">NSPCA’s animal-poisons guidance</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

