  <section id="wildlife-faq" class="section-compact section-rule" aria-labelledby="animals-faq-title" data-pagefind-body>
    <div class="shell animals-facts-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">From the original Animals page</p>
        <h2 id="animals-faq-title">Wildlife questions.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="article-body animals-faq">
        <p class="notice">The following topics are preserved from UnPoison’s previous website. Handling a bird of prey can be dangerous to both you and the bird. Contact a licensed wildlife rehabilitator or vet first. The former home-treatment claim is discussed below for transparency; it is not a treatment recommendation.</p>
        <details>
          <summary>How to approach and capture a poisoned owl, raptor or bird of prey</summary>
          <div>
            <p><strong>Observe before approaching.</strong> The original guide says to attempt capture only if a rehabilitator instructs you, and only if the bird is grounded and unable to fly. Possible signs include not flying away, lethargy, poor coordination, seizures, head tilting, bleeding from the beak, fluffed feathers or half-closed eyes.</p>
            <p>It says immediate containment may be needed if the bird cannot fly and is in danger from traffic, dogs, people or rain, while help is on the way. The guide describes using thick gloves, a large towel and a ventilated cardboard box; approaching slowly from behind; covering the bird’s wings and head with the towel; holding the wings gently against its body without forcing them open; and placing the bird, towel included, into the box. Keep the box quiet, dark and indoors.</p>
            <p><strong>Do not</strong> give food or water, force-feed, allow children or pets near the bird, or leave it in a hot car or direct sun. The original page warns that giving water by mouth can cause aspiration.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How to transport an injured owl, raptor or bird of prey</summary>
          <div>
            <p>The original guide says to take the bird to a rehabilitation facility as soon as possible. During transport, keep the box stable, dark and quiet, and avoid loud noises or sudden movements. Arrange transport with a rehabilitator or veterinarian where possible.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How to administer a homeopathic treatment to a poisoned bird of prey</summary>
          <div>
            <p><strong>Needs clinical review — not a treatment recommendation.</strong> The WordPress page proposed Arsenicum album 200C and gave a method for applying diluted drops to a bird’s beak. This claim and procedure have been retained for editorial review, but we have not found evidence that homeopathy treats rodenticide poisoning. Do not delay veterinary or rehabilitation care to try a home remedy.</p>
            <p>The original cautions against squirting fluid into the mouth or tilting the head back because of aspiration risk. A veterinarian must determine treatment based on the suspected poison; vitamin K1 is used for anticoagulant rodenticides, but not all rodenticides work the same way.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <p>Have a question about the campaign or this guidance? <a href="/contact/">Contact UnPoison</a>. The original page also had a contact form; the local draft routes enquiries through the site’s contact page.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
